\f1\fs22 Chip Stockwell wasn\'92t passed over for promotion fifteen times; he refused every time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He loathed the notion of office work, with the distress of a cat in a creek. He\'92d rather a coffin than the drab embrace of cheese-grey partitioning surrounding a flat-pack stand-up desk, a drooping jaundiced lamp, and a radiation-spewing computer screen. Knowing an office was an inescapable component of any higher-ranking cog-in-the-wheels position he staunchly rejected the notion. As the parade of overseers became more insistent, strident, desperate and psychasthenic, Chip devised schemes of mischief to sabotage his own career prospects without landing himself a dreaded reassignment. Outright dismissal was off the table, for as a certified member of the Global Stasis Technicians Association, he was privy to far too many secrets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which is how he discovered the most optimal activism for his goals: pissing off the Personnel Department. They deserved everything they got, he reasoned. They were all a gaggle of officious gatekeepers, educated in nothing more than the psychological profiling of teddy bears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One time, Chip applied for five months leave in lieu of untaken tea breaks. On winning, he immediately applied to sacrifice the benefit for computer equipment. Once in possession of the new tech, he deliberately and continually sabotaged it so he could harass the IT department with vexatious support tickets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip was kiln of a man with red-as-bricks cheeks, red-as-fire hair, and amber-as-ale eyes, who lumbered around in such a way that he appeared he might crumble, if not for the overstretched balloon of his overalls keeping him contained. His pockets groaned, his buttons screeched, and his plumber\'92s crack grinned at anyone unfortunate enough to look at it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip abhorred the notion of a desk job because he loved his work as a field agent for the company. There was not another job in the world that set such a low bar for skills as a domestic appliance mechanic while bestowing on him the power to arrest any homemaker\'97including their spouses, children, nannies and live-in grannies\'97who tampered with the company\'92s products.\
