\f1\fs22 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip puffed like a blacksmith\'92s bellows as he hauled his industrial carcass up two flights of stairs, passing Unit 15 on the way to Unit 17, where he knocked like an avalanche of bush rock on the door. Conversation and domestic noises from within ceased, as if a broadcaster had been switched off. Presently, the door opened and exposed a meekly terrified woman standing compliant, as if awaiting sentence in a dock. In one hand, she held a spray-bottle of detergent, and in the other, and absorbent rag.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Missus Chuglingham?\'92 Chip asked through acid-washed vocal chords.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The woman spasmed a resigned nod as she looked to the floor and polished Chip\'92s boots with her gaze.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m Chip Stockwell, Stasis Anomalies Investigator from Fridge Force, ma\'92am.\'92 Chip tilted the badge clipped to his breast pocket, as if he might blind Missus Chuglingham with reflected light. She flinched. \'91There\'92s been a complaint,\'92 he said, and checked his clip board. \'91A smell.\'92 His plumber\'92s butt-crack itched, so he scratched it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham blushed with shame. \'91Yes. We\'92re terribly sorry. You see, we\'92ve been on vacation \'85 for three weeks! \'85 and when we returned, only this morning \'85 well \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip sniffed the air. \'91Did you leave perishables out of your stasis unit?\'92 he asked, using the technically more accurate term for what colloquially passed as a refrigerator in this mouldy universe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Most technology in this universe was more backward than any other in the multiverse. In the few cases where its tech was more advanced, it was always weaponised for such horrifically evil purposes, it would have been better if it never existed. Take for instance <<insert instance here>>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which is why stasis technology was such a stand-out. Not only was it astounding for its ability to preserve any degradable material, seemingly forever, it verged on the unbelievable that it was deployed for such universal good. Every household, shack, tent, yurt, igloo, cave and treehouse had a stasis unit, in which all manner of perishables could be kept in perfect condition until needed. Nothing inside the unit would not deteriorate in any way. It was if, inside the unit, time had stopped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While, stasis units were the greatest leap forward in food hygiene in the entire multiverse, they did little or nothing to manage their internal temperature up or down. Which meant kitchens everywhere included the more multi-versally conventional refrigerator (known here as a chilling fridge) in which compartments maintained arbitrary temperatures for keeping beer cold, ice cream frozen and your underwear delightfully invigorating, if you were into that kind of thing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Stasis technology also spawned some cultural oddities. Take, for example, the case of The Knights of the Blessed Order of the Blown Kleenex\'97an offshoot of the Veterans Association of Christmas, Wisconsin, whose annual Cough It Up Pageant would attract cranky old geezers from hundreds of kilometres around. Not content with bragging over cheap bourbons at the local club about 
\f3\i You shoulda seen what I coughed up this morning
\f1\i0 , patrons began preserving their especially gross expectorants in their stasis cupboards, and take them to particularly gag-worthy show-and-tell evenings every third Thursday of the month, where each contestant would, in turns determined by who could blow the loudest nose, carefully unfold their preserved used tissues. Whichever specimen was judged the most offensive (judged on such qualities as appearance, texture, viscosity, aroma and, sometimes, taste) would win the proud kleenex hoarder free drinks at the bar until the following meeting. News of the contest\'97or 
\f3\i pageant
\f1\i0  as it came to be known
\f3\i \'97
\f1\i0 spread faster than a regurgitated virus, and similar contests egested in towns far and wide, especially in the winter months. Within a few years, the practice had become so wide-spread, hardly a town in the Midwest could be inoculated against its popularity. And so the first state-wide Cough It Up Pageant was held, where the elderly and sickly clamoured to win the coveted Golden Tissue Box. Successful these were until Pageant the Fifth, when a seasoned contestant brought a sample he\'92d been keeping in his stasis cupboard since before the last annual contest. Whether by the sample\'92s extended time in stasis (unlikely), or by some extraordinary environmental conditions in transit to the pageant (the flu had been bad that year), the specimen mutated into a new organism so virulent, it was 100% fatal, dissolving the eyes, duodenum and left kneecap of every host it invaded. Not even brown dogs were immune. Within an hour of contracting the virus, hapless victims would be blindly limping in circles as their insides imploded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Let that be a lesson for everyone: no matter how beneficent a technology may seem, never store your snot in a stasis unit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Did you leave perishables out of your stasis unit?\'92 Chip repeated, less kindly which, in no way should imply he was kind the first time he asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No \'85 there\'92s something wrong \'85\'92\'97and here Missus Chuglingham swallowed hard and blanched as she chewed her lip and sagged her shoulders\'97\'91with the stasis unit.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip straightened and puffed out his breast. \'91Ma\'92am, that is quite impossible.\'92 
\f3\i They always try to blame the stasis init, but the stasis unit is perfect. 
\f1\i0 This was serious business. Chip couldn\'92t afford to believe the woman\'92s story. \'91I must inspect the stasis unit. Please stand aside.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham hesitated, just long enough for Chip to justify pulling his weapon which, in any other universe, would appear comical, as it appeared to be a stubby popgun with a plunger stuffed in its muzzle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You don\'92t want to make me glue you to the wall, ma\'92am,\'92 Chip warned, and sneered behind his somewhat limp gunsight, as he visualised Missus Chuglingham glued helplessly to a wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From inside the apartment, a metallic clang like pots falling, punctuated the tension. Missus Chuglingham gasped, covered her mouth, squeezed her eyelids shut against tears, and ran down the hallway. Chip pursued her into the kitchen, where he discovered a man scooping bottles, packets and containers of ruined food into a large garbage sack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Step away from the vegetables,\'92 Chip ordered, as he pulled a respiratory mask from his pocket and looped it one-handed from both his ears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man did as he was told, and held his rubber-gloved hands up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind the mask, Chip kept his mouth closed and held his breath as he peered into the garbage sack on the floor. \'91This is evidence-tampering,\'92 Chip accused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is it?\'92 the man asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know it is, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But we\'92ve done nothing wrong,\'92 the man said as he hugged his wife, and she tried to brush away his putrid gloves from her dress and bare arms. \'91We\'92ve been away, and we came home to\'85this!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham almost wretched into the sack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing wrong, hey?\'92 Chip challenged as he tapped a black scuff mark on the floor with the tip of his boot. \'91Your stasis unit has been moved.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Chuglinghams regarded the rubber stain with horror. \'91We didn\'92t move it,\'92 Missus said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grimaced, nodded and peered down his nose at the terrified couple. \'91Let\'92s see what we have here,\'92 he said, and proceeded to juggle the fridge out of its cabinet recess. Moving it became easier as its feet slid over smears of rotting food on the floor. Chip swivelled it around so he could examine its back. With all the athleticism of an arthritic macaque, he kneeled and peered into the mechanical guts of the unit. What he saw alarmed him: the safety seal of the block box had been snapped and there were scratch marks around the rim of its lid. With the aid of proprietary spanners and screw drivers, Chip dislodged the box, held it reverently in his hands for a few moments, exhaled a deep breath, and cracked the lid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Holy gravity fuck!\'92 he swore. He rose and stood over the quivering couple. \'91Where is it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Where is what?\'92 the Mr Chuglingham asked, stricken with fear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The\'97\'92 Chip started and managed to choke down the rest of his sentence. To cover he strode into the hall, looked down its length, noted open doorways, figured three were bedrooms. \'91You have children?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham cried out. \'91Please! Leave them out of it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How many?\'92 Chip insisted with a good dose of threat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Two!\'92 Mr Chuglingham sputtered, knowing it was best to cooperate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Where are they? School?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Chuglinghams said nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which school?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tregear Public,\'92 Mr Chuglingham confessed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip un-pocketed his tablet, tapped its face. It emitted a dull ring as Chip put it to his ear. \'91I need an immediate collection. Tregear Public School. Chuglingham. Two.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the Chuglinghams whimpered, Chip handcuffed them. \'91I\'92m detaining you for violations of the 
\f3\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But we\'92re innocent!\'92 Mr Chuglingham said through half-choked sobs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ll get your day in arbitration, after you and your family have served your time in a housekeeping reeducation camp.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But those camps are offshore!\'92 Missus Chuglingham wailed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s a matter of national security,\'92 Chip said disingenuously.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Trying to clean out a fridge is a matter of national security?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know it is, ma\'92am.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip called for a back-up team to collect the Chuglinghams. On the way out, he thought he could hear sobbing as he passed Unit 15. He shrugged and thought nothing more of it. Inside, a family grieved the loss of a father and husband who inexplicably committed suicide\'97one Travis Teh.
